# Go Web Platform Surface Rebranding

## Context

Phase 1 changes the identity users and operators see from Skaia to **Go Web
Platform (GWP)** without changing any machine contract. It creates an independently
releasable baseline before module, protocol, storage, database, Docker, repository,
and deployment identifiers move in phase 2.

Surface work must respect tenant branding. SkaiaCraft names, customer domains,
tenant-configured titles, email identities, legal text, logos, favicons, and
content are not platform branding and must not be globally replaced.

## Goal

- Show `Go Web Platform` or `GWP` consistently in platform-owned UI, metadata,
  default emails, health messages, documentation, examples, and approved assets.
- Turn the default `get-started` custom page into a credible, responsive tour of
  the platform's page building, data, community, realtime, interactive, content,
  media, commerce, and extensibility capabilities.
- Preserve all APIs, packages, serialized values, storage keys, protocols,
  infrastructure names, deployment behavior, and repository coordinates.
- Produce a verified surface-only release distinct from later breaking changes.

## Non-Goals

- Renaming Go/npm packages, imports, symbols, protobuf packages, gRPC methods,
  WebSocket subprotocols, JWT issuers, headers, storage keys, CSS identifiers,
  datasource globals, serialized markers, or test domains.
- Renaming images, containers, networks, volumes, database objects, temporary or
  service paths, repositories/remotes, or the local workspace.
- Renaming Grengo, SkaiaCraft, or tenant-owned branding/content.
- Inventing permanent visual assets without approved inputs.
- Advertising unavailable tenant features or replacing an administrator's edited
  `get-started` page/homepage selection.

## Steps

1. Build a surface allowlist. Classify candidates as platform-visible copy,
   tenant-owned value, internal contract, historical evidence, or phase-2 work.
2. Replace platform-owned frontend copy: default title, accessible labels,
   descriptive text, settings/about/help surfaces, errors, empty states, and
   rendered `SKAIA` artwork. Keep tenant configuration authoritative.
3. Replace backend-visible defaults and responses: fallback email sender/TOTP
   display name, health/status prose, and public resource descriptions. Do not
   change claims, headers, logs, cache keys, or other parsed values.
4. Apply approved GWP logo, favicon, and social artwork where defaults exist;
   verify tenant assets still win. Record unavailable assets as blocked.
5. Redesign the seeded `get-started` custom page as a concise capability tour:
   - lead with a GWP hero and clear paths for visitors and administrators;
   - demonstrate representative native blocks instead of only describing the
     builder, using the canonical document model and registered section types;
   - cover visual page building and reusable sections, datasources and derived
     content, documentation/media, forms/polls/surveys/Q&A/voting, community and
     forum workflows, realtime presence/chat/voice/video, commerce, events,
     rankings/rewards, integrations, and tenant branding/security where supported;
   - organize the page as a guided story rather than an exhaustive feature dump,
     with scannable proof points, accessible headings, responsive layouts, and
     calls to action appropriate to the viewer;
   - source claims from implemented specs/registries. Link only to universally
     available public routes unless an existing block already supports feature/
     permission visibility; describe or omit feature-dependent capabilities
     instead of adding a new conditional-page contract or exposing a broken/
     forbidden destination.
6. Preserve existing tenants while distributing the improved page:
   - update the guarded migration `002` insert as the fresh-install seed
     authority and add a forward, additive seed revision for deployed tenants;
     rerunning `002` must remain a no-op when the slug already exists;
   - update only the exact known legacy seed document (or another deterministic
     pristine signature) and never key safety on slug/title alone;
   - preserve customized `get-started` content, its ownership/visibility, and the
     current `landing_page_slug`; administrators with a diverged page retain the
     ordinary page builder as the sole editing/copying authority rather than
     receiving a one-off template refresh subsystem;
   - keep the update idempotent and preserve interactive records. Because the
     SQL bridge runs outside the page service, deploy it before the backend/
     frontend release and purge only the tenant's exact `/` and
     `/page/get-started` SEO keys; do not imply that SQL emits realtime events.
7. Add focused seed/document tests covering clean install, pristine legacy
   upgrade, customized-page preservation, repeated migration, valid registered
   sections, canonical routes, feature/permission-sensitive actions, sanitization,
   and render/edit/save/reload behavior at desktop and mobile widths.
8. Update README, setup, operations, in-app docs, examples, screenshots, and
   current spec/routine prose. Preserve historical evidence, tenant examples,
   and commands whose old identifiers remain true until phase 2.
9. Add focused assertions that visible platform output uses GWP and machine
   identifiers remain byte-for-byte unchanged. Regenerate documentation/search
   artifacts only through their authoritative generators.
10. Release phase 1 independently and record public, Getting Started, upgrade,
    and tenant-branding evidence.

## Verification

- Run relevant backend/root tests and builds plus frontend type-check/build,
  tests, lint, and repository UI policy gates.
- Snapshot machine identity before/after phase 1 and prove no module/import,
  protobuf, WS, JWT, header, browser/Redis, serialized, database, Docker, service,
  or Git coordinate changed.
- Inspect UI, auth/TOTP/email, health/status, page-builder descriptions, errors,
  docs, SEO/social metadata, and downloads with defaults and a branded tenant.
- On a clean database, verify `get-started` is valid GWP content and remains the
  configured landing page. On upgrade fixtures, verify only the pristine legacy
  seed changes; customized content and homepage selection remain byte-for-byte
  unchanged, and a second run is a no-op.
- Render the Getting Started page as guest, member, and administrator with
  representative feature combinations. Assert there are no forbidden/broken
  calls to action, unsupported section types, leaked edit controls, layout
  overflows, inaccessible headings/actions, or duplicated interactive records.
- Search visible output for unexplained `Skaia`; retain a categorized backlog of
  phase-2, tenant, and historical matches.
- Run `git diff --check` and record the phase-1 release identifier in the tip.

